# Genuinely CYCLIC vertical constraint — the case a dogleg resolves.
#
# Two buses cross the same horizontal M4 channel, each connecting a block ABOVE
# the channel at one column and a block BELOW it at the other column, with the
# above/below assignment SWAPPED between the two columns:
#
#   bus x: A_top (col X≈90, above)  ->  A_bot (col Y≈200, below)
#   bus y: B_bot (col X≈90, below)  ->  B_top (col Y≈200, above)
#
# On the shared M5 columns this forces contradictory trunk orderings:
#   column X: x-stub grows down, y-stub grows up  -> x-trunk must sit ABOVE y-trunk
#   column Y: x-stub grows down, y-stub grows up  -> x-trunk must sit BELOW y-trunk
# A 2-cycle in the vertical-constraint graph that no single track ordering of the
# two M4 trunks can satisfy.  resolve_corner_overlaps gives up (its !new_edge
# guard); the cure is a DOGLEG splitting one trunk across two tracks (high for one
# column, low for the other) joined by a short M5 jog.  See
# docs/future/nuts_packing_gaps.md §3.
#

def_layer 4 M4 H TOP 52.94
def_layer 5 M5 V TOP 50.00
def_track_pattern 4 -400  POWER 4 1  SIGNAL 2 1  SIGNAL 2 1 SIGNAL 2 1  SIGNAL 2 1  GROUND 4 1  SIGNAL 2 1 SIGNAL 2 1 SIGNAL 2 1  SIGNAL 2 1
def_track_pattern 5    0  POWER 3 1  SIGNAL 2 1  SIGNAL 2 1 SIGNAL 2 1  SIGNAL 2 1  GROUND 3 1  SIGNAL 2 1 SIGNAL 2 1 SIGNAL 2 1  SIGNAL 2 1

add_block A_top  80 150 100 200
add_block B_bot  80   0 100  50
add_block A_bot 190   0 210  50
add_block B_top 190 150 210 200

add_bus x[4]  A_top.o A_bot.i
add_bus y[4]  B_bot.o B_top.i

run_bundler strict

corner_margin dy 2 dx 2
set_min_stub_length 2
generate_topologies
check_design topo

# force the cyclic corner overlap (Z_VHV through the channel for both buses)
select_topologies 1,2 4
run_planner 1

run_nuts
check_design nuts
run_detailed_nuts
check_design dnuts

visualize
